@title From Register to Stock Count
@subtitle How a store sale reaches the online stock count at Halsted Home Goods
@author Retail Platform
@date 2026-10-08

!!! The Stock Ledger is the only source of truth for stock. The Storefront count may lag the ledger by up to 20 seconds.

# Sale-to-stock sequence
diagram sale sequence title="Sale to stock count"
sale: pos "Store POS" svc
sale: agent "Edge Sync Agent" svc
sale: api "Inventory API" svc
sale: ledger "Stock Ledger" db
sale: store "Storefront" svc
sale: pos -> agent : sale recorded, 2 units of SKU 48113
sale: agent -> api : POST /adjustments, batched every 5 s
sale: api -> ledger : append decrement
sale: ledger --> api : new on-hand count
sale: api --> agent : 202 Accepted
sale: api -> store : publish stock change
sale: opt "store link lost"
sale: pos -> agent : keeps selling
sale: note agent : holds adjustments in local queue
sale: agent -> api : replay queued adjustments
sale: end

When the store loses its connection, the Store POS keeps selling and the Edge Sync Agent keeps the adjustments in a local queue. When the link returns, it sends them to the Inventory API in order.

# Timing budget
table = hop,limit; Register to agent,under 1 s; Agent batch interval,5 s; API to ledger,under 200 ms; Ledger to storefront,under 10 s; **End to end (p99 of sales)**,**under 20 s**

# Good to know
- The local queue holds up to 4 hours of sales, about 3,000 adjustments at a busy store.
- Adjustments carry an idempotency key, so replays never double count.
- Online orders reserve stock for 15 minutes at checkout.
